(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2800010400-itonami-install-path-and-hyakka-repair")
  (:adr/title "itonami.cloud install path (curl + kotoba build), and repairing nine files a resident bot left unreadable")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-02")
  (:adr/body "## What this session did

Owner asked for a curl install route on itonami.cloud and for the install
steps to be carried in llms.txt, then added that `kotoba` CLI / `kotoba build`
come first so that path had to be made sound too. On `next` the owner selected
all four follow-ups: cut a kotoba release, drain the new dependency finding,
extend the install funnel, and take a repo-bot floor finding.

## Landed (all on default branches, all deployed where applicable)

### 1. itonami.cloud install path — network-awai/cloud-itonami @ 2c3a4b0d867f

- `/install.sh` returns **302 to kotoba-lang/kotoba's installer**, so
  `curl -fsSL https://itonami.cloud/install.sh | sh` works. A redirect, not a
  copy: the upstream installer verifies the release archive's published
  SHA-256 before unpacking, and a second copy here would drift silently while
  still looking installable.
- **The defect this closed:** before the rule, `/install.sh` matched the SPA
  catch-all and returned the home page **with HTTP 200**. Piping that into
  `sh` is the failure mode the rule exists to remove.
- `public/docs/install.md` — three routes (CLI, desktop app, no install at
  all), each with the manual checksum form, plus an \"After it opens\" section
  naming /isco-1212/ and /onboarding/.
- `public/llms.txt` — an `## Install` section carrying the same content in
  agent form.
- Home install strip — a CLI column with the one-liner, and an app-lane link.
  en + ja written; the other five locales fall back to :en by design.

Measured end to end against the published route, not transcribed: the
one-liner installed into an isolated prefix, and that binary then ran
`rad new` -> `test` -> `build` to a 94-byte hello.wasm.

### 2. kotoba rad --project resolution — kotoba-lang/kotoba @ eca2bead5ec3

`cd hello && kotoba build` failed with `:wasm/source-not-readable
./src/kotoba_app.kotoba`: with no `--project`, `rad-adapter/project-name`
fell back to the literal `kotoba-app` because \".\" has no last path segment.
The README that `rad new` SCAFFOLDS tells the reader to run `--project .`, so
the scaffold's own instructions did not work. The host now resolves an
absent/`.`/`./` project to the canonical path of `user.dir` before the pure
planner sees it; an explicit project passes through byte-identical.

The test pins the resulting PATH, not \"it did not throw\" — the bug was a
wrong path reported as a successful plan. Control: with the resolution
replaced by the identity it used to be, the test fails 5 of 8 assertions, on
those assertions.

### 3. app-hyakka repair — network-awai/app-hyakka @ 88a138dbaa73

Came for one vulnerable package, found the build broken on main since
2026-08-30 with a resident bot still committing over it.

- `decode-uri-component@0.2.2` (GHSA-vcc3-ghjq-m6fr) reached production via
  `css@^3.0.0` -> `source-map-resolve@^0.2.0`. `css` and `html` are declared
  runtime dependencies with **zero imports anywhere** in the repo, so the fix
  is deletion, not a bump. `datascript` stays — it is imported.
- **Nine files repaired**, all one shape: content appended after the form it
  belonged to had already closed. A set closed before its last element; a
  vector closed and appended to four more times; `(defn ...)` spliced into
  another defn's head so two collectors shared one body; a deftest truncated
  mid-expression; `:sources` entries left open when the next key was written.
  Introduced by `hyakka-source-scout`, one per run, from 4309f84f.
- `scripts/read_check.clj` reads all 1,305 source and data files with the
  ACTUAL reader, wired ahead of `npm test`. It uses the reader rather than a
  bracket counter deliberately: the counter written first called a file
  balanced that the reader rejects. Registers `#js` (without which seven
  healthy .cljs files report unreadable and hide the real finding); exits 2
  on an empty scan.

`npm run build` completes (121 files, 0 warnings).

## Not done, and why — these are the resume points

### G1. kotoba v0.7.4 is NOT released. main is 18 commits ahead of v0.7.3.

The `--project` fix therefore does NOT reach anyone installing through curl
or Homebrew; both still deliver v0.7.3, and /docs/install.md says so.

Blocked on platform coverage, not on the toolchain: GraalVM is now available
at `~/.local/share/graalvm/graalvm-jdk-21.0.12+7.1` (extracted from
Homebrew's download cache without sudo — the cask itself FAILS, it needs sudo
for /Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines and then exits 0 anyway after its purge
step). **Only darwin-arm64 is buildable on this machine.** v0.7.3 shipped
three platforms, and install.sh resolves `releases/latest/download`, so
publishing a partial v0.7.4 would 404 for darwin-amd64 and linux-amd64 —
it would break the curl route this same session shipped. A prerelease avoids
that and reaches nobody.

Also required, and not done: `:release/current` lives in
`kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` `lang/version-policy.edn` (a different repo), and
`:release-notes {:require-surface-status-diff? true}` demands a
surface-status diff in the notes.

Next step: identify the builder that produced v0.7.3's darwin-amd64 and
linux-amd64 artifacts (this workspace's fleet is all Apple silicon macOS), or
decide the release is arm64-only and change install.sh's platform handling
first so the other two platforms fail loudly rather than 404.

### G2. app-hyakka `config/knowledge-ingest.edn` is still unreadable, so `npm test` fails.

Same corruption at scale. Partial surgery on it was REVERTED rather than
shipped: a file that looks repaired and still does not parse is worse than
one that plainly does not.

The reason for stopping is measurable: `clojure.edn/read` reports only 95 of
~159 source entries inside the top-level map, while the hand-rolled delimiter
analysis called the file balanced. That analysis was wrong twice in one
session. Repairing it means deciding what each half-written entry was meant
to be — authoring the ingest config, not repairing syntax — and belongs to
whoever owns it.

Next step: `cd orgs/network-awai/app-hyakka && clojure -M scripts/read_check.clj`
names it. Decide per truncated entry: complete it from its own kind's
convention, or drop it (it has never taken effect — the config has been
unreadable, so the resident has not run with it).

### G3. There is still no gate between a repo bot's commit and main.

`read_check.clj` lives in app-hyakka and runs under `npm test`; the bot does
not run `npm test`, which is exactly how nine broken files reached main. The
check that would actually stand in the way is a fleet gate.

Not written. The obstacle is real and worth stating: `:nbb-script` gates run
under nbb (ClojureScript), which has no reader with `:read-cond`, and a
delimiter-balance checker is the weaker check that already proved
untrustworthy here. `:jvm-test` needs a `:test` alias app-hyakka does not
have. Registration point is `scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn` — app-hyakka already
has one gate there (`hyakka-catalog-freshness`), which did not catch this
because it checks catalog freshness, not readability.

### G4. `kysely@0.27.6` in cloud-itonami/media — PRODUCTION, reachability `unused-direct`.

Declared but zero imports, same shape as the css/html finding just fixed, so
the remedy is deletion rather than a bump. Three advisories:
GHSA-8cpq-38p9-67gx, GHSA-pv5w-4p9q-p3v2, GHSA-wmrf-hv6w-mr66.

### G5. The SessionStart detector hook prints a command that does not exist.

It prints `nbb --classpath \".:scripts/nbb_compat\" scripts/dependency-vulnerabilities.cljs`.
That file is not in the repo and not on origin/main. The real detector, per
`manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` `:dependency-vulnerabilities` `:argv`, is
`nbb scripts/gen-dependency-inventory.cljs --osv --findings`. Every other
detector line in that hook should be checked the same way.

## Corrections made during the session

Nine `backend_qualification_test` failures were first reported as
\"pre-existing\" in kotoba. They were **environmental** — every one was an
`evidence exists: ../kotoba-lang/... | ../amu/... | ../kototama/...`
assertion failing because the /tmp worktree had no sibling repos. All nine
files are present in the real checkout, and re-running there gives 118 tests
/ 640 assertions / 0 failures. Same class as the `deploy_site_test` failures,
which ARE environmental (sibling `net-kotobase/ipfs` is not checked out in
this workspace at all).

## Traps re-confirmed, for the next agent

- **A generated file that regenerates SMALLER is stale, not fixed.**
  `public/ops/index.html` was reverted twice: regenerating it from a /tmp
  worktree drops whole yakuwari role entries, because the worktree lacks the
  data that page reads.
- **v0.4.1 is Cloud Itonami's latest STABLE.** The 0.5.x tags are
  pre-releases and GitHub's `latest` resolves to v0.4.1, so the existing
  download links were correct. Nearly \"fixed\" as a stale version.
- **A /tmp worktree breaks sibling `../<repo>` paths**, which shows up as
  test failures and `:local/root` classpath errors that look like real
  breakage. Symlink the org dirs beside the worktree, or run in a tree whose
  parent has them.
- **`brew install --cask` can exit 0 without installing** when its sudo step
  fails; the purge step's status is what you get.")))
